from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "9. What the Letters to the Body Do with Sickness"),
("p", "Claim: the letters written to the Body mention sickness often and plainly, and in none of them is a healing ministry given, an authority to heal laid on anyone, or healing promised. Sickness is met with prayer, with ordinary care, with the company of a physician, with patience and with the mercy of God. This chapter reads the passages in the order of the letters and records what each says and what each leaves unsaid. Volume 1 reads three of them briefly in chapter 9.4, and this chapter reads them in full with the rest."),
("sub", "9.1 Prayer for everything"),
("p", "Paul’s instruction for anxiety is a command to pray. “Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God. And the peace of God, which passeth all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus” (Philippians 4:6, 7). The command covers “every thing.” The result named is the peace of God. The letter does not say that every request will be granted, and it does not set sickness apart from other requests. Paul writes also, “Praying always with all prayer and supplication in the Spirit, and watching thereunto with all perseverance and supplication for all saints” (Ephesians 6:18), and “Pray without ceasing” (1 Thessalonians 5:17)."),
("p", "Paul asks for prayer for himself in the same way. He writes to the Philippians, “For I know that this shall turn to my salvation through your prayer, and the supply of the Spirit of Jesus Christ” (Philippians 1:19), and to the Corinthians he writes of his trouble in Asia, “in whom we trust that he will yet deliver us; Ye also helping together by prayer for us” (2 Corinthians 1:10, 11). The Body prays, and it prays for the deliverance of its members. The letters do not tie that prayer to a gift or an office."),
("sub", "9.2 Epaphroditus"),
("p", "The most direct account of an illness in the letters is the account of Epaphroditus. “Yet I supposed it necessary to send to you Epaphroditus, my brother, and companion in labour, and fellowsoldier, but your messenger, and he that ministered to my wants. For he longed after you all, and was full of heaviness, because that ye had heard that he had been sick. For indeed he was sick nigh unto death: but God had mercy on him; and not on him only, but on me also, lest I should have sorrow upon sorrow” (Philippians 2:25–27)."),
("p", "The passage says what Paul’s fellow worker suffered: he “was sick nigh unto death.” It says what followed: “God had mercy on him.” It says that the mercy was also to Paul, “lest I should have sorrow upon sorrow.” It does not say that Paul healed him, that he prayed for him, that the church prayed, or that a gift was used. Paul then asks the church to “Receive him therefore in the Lord with all gladness; and hold such in reputation: Because for the work of Christ he was nigh unto death” (Philippians 2:29, 30). The passage records a recovery and attributes it to the mercy of God. The volume reads it as the letter writes it: God had mercy."),
("sub", "9.3 Timothy’s stomach and Trophimus"),
("p", "Paul writes to Timothy, “Drink no longer water, but use a little wine for thy stomach’s sake and thine often infirmities” (1 Timothy 5:23). The sentence is an instruction about the care of the body. It names a condition, “thine often infirmities,” and gives a remedy, a little wine. It does not say that Timothy should seek a healing, that Paul will pray for him or that the elders should be called."),
("p", "In the last letter Paul writes, “Erastus abode at Corinth: but Trophimus have I left at Miletum sick” (2 Timothy 4:20). Trophimus is a man Paul had known from Ephesus and who had come with him to Jerusalem. The sentence says that Paul left him sick. It gives no reason, and it does not say that Paul prayed for him or healed him. The verse reports the fact. Volume 1 reads both passages in chapter 9.4 and reports that no healing is recorded in either. This chapter takes the same silence into the larger pattern."),
("sub", "9.4 Luke the physician"),
("p", "Paul’s letters name a physician among his companions. “Luke, the beloved physician, and Demas, greet you” (Colossians 4:14). Near the end of his life Paul writes, “Only Luke is with me” (2 Timothy 4:11). The passages record that the company of the apostle included a physician, and that in the last letter the physician was the one who remained. They do not say what Luke did for the sick. They do show that the Body’s letters name a physician without comment and without any contrast between his work and prayer."),
("sub", "9.5 The infirmity of Paul’s flesh"),
("p", "Paul reminds the Galatians of his first visit. “Ye know how through infirmity of the flesh I preached the gospel unto you at the first. And my temptation which was in my flesh ye despised not, nor rejected; but received me as an angel of God, even as Christ Jesus” (Galatians 4:13, 14). The passage names an infirmity of the flesh as the circumstance of his first preaching. It says what the Galatians did: they did not despise or reject him. It does not say what the infirmity was, whether it was healed or whether he sought healing, and the volume does not guess."),
("sub", "9.6 Sickness at Corinth"),
("p", "Paul writes of sickness in the assembly at Corinth, in the passage on the Lord’s table. “For he that eateth and drinketh unworthily, eateth and drinketh damnation to himself, not discerning the Lord’s body. For this cause many are weak and sickly among you, and many sleep. For if we would judge ourselves, we should not be judged. But when we are judged, we are chastened of the Lord, that we should not be condemned with the world” (1 Corinthians 11:29–32). The passage links the weakness and sickness at Corinth to the abuse of the table and says what the church should do: judge itself. The remedy named is self-judgment, not a call to the gifts of healing that Paul lists in the next chapter but one. The volume reports the order and the remedy. It does not say that all sickness at Corinth was of this kind."),
("sub", "9.7 Groaning in the body"),
("p", "The letters speak of the body’s weakness as the common condition of the Body. “Likewise the Spirit also helpeth our infirmities: for we know not what we should pray for as we ought: but the Spirit itself maketh intercession for us with groanings which cannot be uttered” (Romans 8:26). The word “infirmities” there is the same root as the words for the sick in the other passages. The verse says that the Spirit helps in the weakness by interceding. A few verses earlier Paul says, “And not only they, but ourselves also, which have the firstfruits of the Spirit, even we ourselves groan within ourselves, waiting for the adoption, to wit, the redemption of our body” (Romans 8:23). The body’s redemption is waited for and not yet given."),
("p", "He says the same to the Corinthians. “For which cause we faint not; but though our outward man perish, yet the inward man is renewed day by day” (2 Corinthians 4:16). And he writes of the house of this tabernacle, “For in this we groan, earnestly desiring to be clothed upon with our house which is from heaven” (2 Corinthians 5:2). To the Philippians he writes of the Saviour, “Who shall change our vile body, that it may be fashioned like unto his glorious body” (Philippians 3:21). In these passages the body’s weakness is the condition in which the Body waits, and the hope is the change of the body at the Saviour’s coming."),
("tbl", "What the letters to the Body do with sickness.", [
    ("Philippians 4:6", T("Phil 4:6"), "Prayer in everything, with thanksgiving."),
    ("Philippians 2:27", T("Phil 2:27"), "Epaphroditus was sick nigh unto death; God had mercy."),
    ("1 Timothy 5:23", T("1 Tim 5:23"), "A little wine for the stomach and the often infirmities."),
    ("2 Timothy 4:20", T("2 Tim 4:20"), "Trophimus left at Miletum sick."),
    ("Colossians 4:14", T("Col 4:14"), "Luke, the beloved physician, is among Paul’s company."),
    ("Romans 8:26", T("Rom 8:26"), "The Spirit helpeth our infirmities."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The letters to the Body command prayer in everything and show Paul asking for it.",
    "Epaphroditus’ recovery is attributed to the mercy of God, and no gift or healing is named.",
    "Timothy is told to use a little wine, Trophimus is left sick and Luke the physician is named without comment.",
    "Sickness at Corinth is linked to the Lord’s table and its remedy is self-judgment.",
    "The body’s weakness is the condition in which the Body waits for the change of the body.",
  ]),
]
